nd the full scope of what's needed. I need to check: are there test files already? The source_code section only shows .go files, not _test.go files. So tests likely don't exist yet.\n\nGiven I can only propose 1 task, I should pick the most foundational one. Per P5 (dependency ordering): \"libraries before CLI\" and \"foundational code before features.\"\n\npkg/testutils is the most foundational — it's the differential testing harness that all cmd/ tests will depend on. Let me propose tests for pkg/testutils.\n\nActually wait — do I have access to the PRDs? The project_context doesn't include the actual PRD content, just references. The source code shows the implementation with PRD requirement references in comments. Let me use those to understand what requirements exist.\n\nLooking at the difftest.go comments, I can see the requirements mapped out: the DiffTest struct, stdin and environment handling, the NormalizeFunc type, the RunDiffTests function, binary execution with timeout support, working directory management, environment variable building, normalizer application, and output comparison across stdout, stderr, and exit codes.\n\nI should propose a single task: write comprehensive tests for pkg/testutils that cover all the differential testing functionality. The stitch agent will have filesystem access to read the actual PRD if needed.\n\nLooking at the requirement structure, I can fit everything into 4 testable areas: DiffTest execution and mechanics, output comparison with divergence reporting, the normalization pipeline, and file-state verification. This keeps the scope manageable while covering the full R1-R5 range from the PRD.\n\nThe stitch agent would need to examine the existing difftest.go implementation, then write a comprehensive test file covering the RunDiffTests function with both passing and divergent cases, the normalizer functions, file comparison logic, environment variable defaults, and timeout handling. I'm estimating around 150-250 lines f