able parsing and stdin truncation can be tested directly within the same package since they're unexported. I think the task to create helper binaries is justified because without them, I can't properly exercise the main entry point.\n\nLet me double-check against the constraint about not inventing design decisions — the `RunDiffTests` signature itself requires two binary paths, so the need for test binaries is directly derived from that API design, not something I'm adding. That makes it valid.\n\nNow I should look at what else needs work in the release. The format use case is still pending.\n\nLooking at the remaining packages, I see implementation exists for format, sys, and testutils, but no test files are shown. Since testutils is the differential testing harness that everything else depends on, testing it first aligns with the foundational-before-features principle.\n\nNow I'm examining the PRD references in the code more carefully — the harness API accepts binary paths, defaults buildEnv to LC_ALL=C, and follows the naming convention of difftest.go → difftest_test.go. Rather than inventing implementation details, I should ground the design decision in what the architecture actually requires: since RunDiffTests takes binary paths as input, the test needs to provide actual binaries to validate the API works correctly. That's a practical constraint derived from the design itself, not an arbitrary choice.\n\nFor the test file itself, I'm sketching out the scope — it would need test cases covering matching output, stdout divergence, stderr divergence, exit code divergence, plus tests for the timestamp normalizer and composed normalizers. That's roughly 150-180 lines of test code total, which gives the stitch agent a concrete sense of what \"comprehensive\" means here.\n\nNow I'm mapping this to the use case identifier rel00.0-uc004-testutils and finalizing the task definition with the PRD and source code as required reading. and unexported functions to be tested)\n